#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""regatta limit — change a fleet limit on one host or all of them, effective on each host's next read:
no build, no landing, no overlay run. Stdlib, Python 3.9.

    regatta limit list [--on H[,H…] | --all]          every limit, each host's value and where it came from
    regatta limit get KEY [--on H[,H…] | --all]       one limit the same way
    regatta limit set KEY H:VALUE [H:VALUE…] --why TEXT          a host's value, written on that host
    regatta limit set KEY VALUE --all --why TEXT                 the same value on every host of the fleet
    regatta limit set KEY LOGIN:VALUE (--on H… | --all) --why TEXT   a login's value (the week ceiling), on those hosts
    regatta limit unset KEY H [H…] | --all --why TEXT            back to what the config says
    regatta limit unset KEY LOGIN (--on H… | --all) --why TEXT
    (set and unset take --over too: see History)

History (limit-history, 2026-11-02). Every set and unset takes `--why TEXT`, one line, kept in its row with the value
the host's layer and config give before and after, the session id when one is set (Claude Code's, else Codex's), and
`over` when `--over` carried it past a refusal. Before it writes, it prints each target's last change: when, from
which host and session, old → new, and its why, the newest of the rows in `limits.jsonl` on every host of the fleet
(each read over the same road; one that cannot be read is said) and the overlay's last commit that changed what
that host reads from the key's row (`<key> <values…>` in the overlay's checkout, the `regatta/overlay` marker, read
with `git log -G` over HEAD and its upstream as last fetched; skipped when no overlay is named, said when one is
named and cannot be read). The write is judged against the newest change from another session (an overlay commit
always is; a row unless its session is this one, or neither has one and it was made from this host), past this
session's own rows and stopping at its own `--over` row; for a login that reads the `*:` line, a `*:` change counts.
Where that change is under 24 h old and this write moves the value the other way (both numbers and the other
direction; where either is no number, any other value), the write is refused with that change quoted (exit 2,
nothing written), unless `--over`; so is a write to a host whose own `limits.jsonl` will not read. An unset that
leaves what the host reads as it was is never held. `get` and `list` end each line with its value's last change.

KEY is a key's whole name (`wharf-runs/run-cap`) or its last part (`run-cap`). The limits are what the
modules declare in their `limits` file (wharf-runs': the run cap, the check slots, the memory headroom,
the load rule; lighthouse's: the week ceiling), each with its shape and its trial read:

  host    a line `<host>:<value>` each host reads for itself (`run-cap`, `headroom`, `check-slots`)
  plain   one value the host reads whole (`load-max`: the rule berth on that host judges every candidate by)
  login   lines `<login>:<value>`, `*:` for any login (`week-max`), the same on every host they are set on

Where it writes (why: live-limits, 2026-11-01). Each host's own config dir, in its live layer:
`<config>/live/<key>`, which the core's config_read reads in front of `<config>/<key>` (common.py's
live_merge: a layer line for a word replaces the config's entry for that word, on whatever line; a plain layer
line replaces the config's plain lines). The overlay's `bootstrap.sh --config` writes `<config>/<key>` and never `live/`, so a
value set here survives it. The trade-off taken: a value lives on each host, written over ssh, so a host
that cannot be reached keeps its old value and is named (exit 3), and a host added later starts from its
config, not from the fleet's last word; the fleet ref (the queue's home) would have reached every host at
its next fetch, but a read of a limit would then wait on the network, and a cap must read in a hook's time.

Refusals. A value is tried before anything is written: the key's reader runs on a scratch config dir that
holds only that value, for the host it is meant for, and must read back the value given (dispatch's `--cap`,
check's `--slots`, headroom's `limits`, berth's `--load-key`, account's `week-max`). A value it reads as
anything else — the default, a parse error — is refused, with what the reader said, and nothing is
written anywhere (exit 2). `DISPATCH_MAX_RUNS` and `CHECK_SLOTS`, which override the files, are dropped
for the trial; they still win on a host that sets them (`get` cannot see a host's environment: `dispatch
--cap` there is the cap's last word).

The fleet: this host and the peers `regatta/fleet-ssh` names (`<host>:<ssh alias>` lines, read by this verb
and by wharf-runs' `peer_cap` for a local user's row, as `wharf-runs/kick-ssh` is the kick's: a scope's node may be named
here and nowhere a placer reads),
else those `wharf-runs/ssh` names. Whoever installs the modules writes it; until the owner's overlay does,
`--all` reaches what `wharf-runs/ssh` names, and every `--all` prints the hosts it reached and names each host
`lighthouse/hosts` knows with no road here. A host is reached with `ssh -o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=10 <alias>` (REGATTA_SSH stands in
for the tests), reads and writes the config dir there (`REGATTA_CONFIG`, else the default), and needs nothing of
regatta installed to be written: its readers honour the layer once its checkout has this change. After a write, and
in `get` and `list`, each host's own reader is asked what it reads, a peer's over the same road through its config
dir's `home` file. After a set, a host whose reader reads another value, refuses, or runs a checkout that predates the
layer is said with exit 3; a peer with no checkout is said and not failed. `get` and `list` show what each reader
reads beside the file's value, and exit 3 only on a host they could not reach or a reader that refuses.
Each change is a row in `limits.jsonl`, in `.local/state/regatta` under the home folder of the host that made it,
and of the host it changed (since limit-history: a host whose fleet cannot reach the maker still holds its history).

`set` and `unset` refuse an unattended caller (REGATTA_UNATTENDED or CLAUDE_UNATTENDED): a dispatched run
refused at its host's cap must not raise the cap itself; the owner's word reaches a host through a session the
owner runs. `list` and `get` read only. A set says each config line its value stands over, whole: a plain key's
`log` word or a headroom line's other rules are not kept unless the value names them.

Exit 0 done; 2 a usage error, a refused value or an unattended caller; 3 a host that could not be reached, one
whose reader reads another value after the write or runs a checkout that predates the layer (the rest done), or, in
`get` and `list`, a reader that refuses the config or could not tell. A reader refuses with exit 2; any other
non-zero exit is said as "could not tell", never as a refusal.
LIMIT_HOST stands in for this host's name (the tests).
"""
import argparse
import datetime as dt
import json
import os
import re
import shlex
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
from pathlib import Path

sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / "hooks"))
import common  # noqa: E402

SHAPES = ("host", "plain", "login")
KEY_RE = re.compile(r"^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*/[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*$")
TIMEOUT = 25
# what overrides a file on the host itself, so a trial read must not inherit it
TRIAL_DROP = ("DISPATCH_MAX_RUNS", "CHECK_SLOTS", "DISPATCH_HOST", "CHECK_HOST", "HEADROOM_HOST")
# a read-back keeps the overrides (they are what the host reads by) and drops only the host stand-ins
HOST_VARS = ("DISPATCH_HOST", "CHECK_HOST", "HEADROOM_HOST")


class Usage(Exception):
    pass


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- the table

def module_dirs():
    """[(module name, folder)]: the regatta checkout's modules, then installed plugins it lacks."""
    root = common.regatta_root()
    seen = []
    if root:
        seen += [(p.name, p) for p in sorted((root / "modules").glob("*")) if p.is_dir()]
    names = {n for n, _ in seen}
    for plugin, base in common._installed_roots():
        name = str(plugin).split("@", 1)[0]   # the cache's folder is a version: the plugin's name says which module
        if base.is_dir() and name not in names:
            names.add(name)
            seen.append((name, base))
    return seen


def table():
    """{key: {shape, default, probe, star, module}} from each module's `limits` file:
    `<key> <shape>[*] <default|-> <probe…>`, the probe run from the module's folder, `{host}` and `{login}`
    its placeholders. A line it cannot read is said on stderr and skipped."""
    out = {}
    for name, m in module_dirs():
        f = m / "limits"
        try:
            lines = f.read_text(encoding="utf-8").splitlines()
        except OSError:
            continue
        for l in lines:
            l = l.strip()
            if not l or l.startswith("#"):
                continue
            parts = l.split(None, 3)
            if len(parts) < 4 or not KEY_RE.match(parts[0]) or parts[1].rstrip("*") not in SHAPES:
                print("regatta limit: %s: a line it cannot read: %s" % (f, l), file=sys.stderr)
                continue
            out[parts[0]] = {"shape": parts[1].rstrip("*"), "star": parts[1].endswith("*"),
                             "default": None if parts[2] == "-" else parts[2], "probe": parts[3], "module": m,
                             "name": name}
    return out


def resolve_key(name, tab):
    if name in tab:
        return name
    hits = [k for k in tab if k.split("/", 1)[1] == name]
    if len(hits) == 1:
        return hits[0]
    raise Usage("no limit %r (regatta limit list names them%s)" % (name, ": " + ", ".join(sorted(hits)) if hits else ""))


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- the fleet

def norm_host(h):
    return re.sub(r"[^a-z0-9-]", "-", (h or "").lower()).strip("-")


def me():
    return norm_host(os.environ.get("LIMIT_HOST") or common.host_name())


def fleet():
    """{host: alias or None (this host)}, this host first."""
    here = me()
    out = {here: None}
    lines = common.config_lines("regatta/fleet-ssh")
    if lines is None:
        lines = common.config_lines("wharf-runs/ssh") or []
    for l in lines:
        h, colon, alias = l.partition(":")
        h, alias = norm_host(h), alias.strip()
        if colon and h and alias and h != here:
            out.setdefault(h, alias)
    return out


def roadless(fl, key=None):
    """The hosts lighthouse/hosts knows, and those this host's lines for KEY name, that have no road from here."""
    named = [norm_host(x) for x in common.config_lines("lighthouse/hosts") or []]
    if key:
        named += [norm_host(w) for w, _ in map(word_of, common.config_lines(key) or []) if w and w != "*"]
    out = []
    for h in named:
        if h and h not in fl and h not in out:
            out.append(h)
    return out


def hosts_of(a, fl, key=None):
    if a.all:
        missing = roadless(fl, key)
        print("regatta limit: --all is %s%s" % (", ".join(fl), "; no road to %s (regatta/fleet-ssh names none)"
                                                 % ", ".join(missing) if missing else ""), file=sys.stderr)
        return list(fl)
    if a.on:
        hs = [norm_host(h) for h in a.on.split(",") if h.strip()]
        bad = [h for h in hs if h not in fl]
        if bad:
            raise Usage("no road to %s: not this host, nor a peer regatta/fleet-ssh (else wharf-runs/ssh) names"
                        % ", ".join(bad))
        return hs
    return list(fl)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- reading and writing a host

def ssh_cmd():
    return shlex.split(os.environ.get("REGATTA_SSH") or "ssh") + ["-o", "BatchMode=yes", "-o", "ConnectTimeout=10"]


REMOTE_DIR = 'c="${REGATTA_CONFIG:-$HOME/.config/regatta}"'


def read_host(host, alias, key):
    """(layer lines or None, base lines or None) on host; raises OSError naming why it could not."""
    return read_host_keys(host, alias, [key])[key]


def read_host_keys(host, alias, keys):
    """{key: (layer, base)} on host, in one ssh for a peer; raises OSError naming why it could not."""
    if alias is None:
        out = {}
        for key in keys:
            layer, lwhy = common._config_file(common.CONFIG / common.LIVE / key)
            base, _ = common._config_file(common.CONFIG / key)
            out[key] = ((None if lwhy else layer), base)
        return out
    parts = [REMOTE_DIR]
    for i, key in enumerate(keys):
        q = shlex.quote(key)
        parts.append('echo @@live%d; cat "$c/live/"%s 2>/dev/null; echo; echo @@base%d; cat "$c/"%s 2>/dev/null; echo'
                     % (i, q, i, q))
    parts.append("echo @@end")
    out = run_ssh(alias, "; ".join(parts))
    if "@@end" not in out:
        raise OSError("no answer from %s" % alias)
    clean = lambda t: [l.strip() for l in t.lstrip("\ufeff").splitlines() if l.strip()] or None
    got = {}
    for i, key in enumerate(keys):
        nxt = "@@live%d\n" % (i + 1) if i + 1 < len(keys) else "@@end"
        live = out.split("@@live%d\n" % i, 1)[1].split("@@base%d\n" % i, 1)[0]
        base = out.split("@@base%d\n" % i, 1)[1].split(nxt, 1)[0]
        got[key] = (clean(live), clean(base))
    return got


def run_ssh(alias, cmd, stdin=None):
    try:
        # no stdin of the caller's: `while read …; do regatta limit set …; done < f` would lose the rest of f to ssh
        r = subprocess.run(ssh_cmd() + ["--", alias, cmd], input=stdin, stdin=None if stdin is not None else subprocess.DEVNULL,
                           capture_output=True, text=True, errors="replace", timeout=TIMEOUT)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as e:
        raise OSError("ssh %s: %s" % (alias, type(e).__name__))
    if r.returncode != 0:
        raise OSError("ssh %s: exit %d %s" % (alias, r.returncode, (r.stderr.strip().splitlines() or [""])[-1][:160]))
    return r.stdout


def write_host(host, alias, key, lines):
    """The host's layer for key becomes lines; none removes it."""
    text = "".join(l + "\n" for l in lines)
    if alias is None:
        f = common.CONFIG / common.LIVE / key
        if not lines:
            try:
                f.unlink()
            except FileNotFoundError:
                pass
            return
        f.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        tmp = f.with_name(".%s.tmp.%d" % (f.name, os.getpid()))
        tmp.write_text(text, encoding="utf-8")
        os.replace(tmp, f)   # a reader mid-write sees the old file or the new one, never an empty one
        return
    if not KEY_RE.match(key):   # the table's keys only, so the names below need no quoting
        raise OSError("no such key: %r" % key)
    d, b = key.rsplit("/", 1)
    if not lines:
        run_ssh(alias, '%s; rm -f "$c/live/%s/%s"' % (REMOTE_DIR, d, b))
        return
    # through a temporary file and a rename, as here: a reader there never sees half a value
    run_ssh(alias, '%s; d="$c/live/%s"; mkdir -p "$d" && cat > "$d/.%s.tmp.$$" && mv -f "$d/.%s.tmp.$$" "$d/%s"'
            % (REMOTE_DIR, d, b, b, b), stdin=text)


STATE = '"$HOME/.local/state/regatta/limits.jsonl"'


def log_file():
    return Path.home() / ".local" / "state" / "regatta" / "limits.jsonl"


def log_on(alias, row):
    """ROW appended to a peer's own limits.jsonl as well, so the host a change was made to holds it wherever it was
    made from: a host whose fleet cannot reach the maker still sees it (round 1). A failure is said, never fatal."""
    try:
        run_ssh(alias, 'mkdir -p "$HOME/.local/state/regatta" && cat >> %s' % STATE,
                stdin=json.dumps(row, sort_keys=True) + "\n")
    except OSError as e:
        print("regatta limit: no log row on %s (%s): its own history lacks this change" % (alias, e), file=sys.stderr)


def log(row):
    f = log_file()
    try:
        f.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        with f.open("a", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            fh.write(json.dumps(row, sort_keys=True) + "\n")
    except OSError as e:
        print("regatta limit: no log row (%s)" % e, file=sys.stderr)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- a key's history
# Why (limit-history, 2026-11-02): a session read a run-cap line the overlay had dropped on the owner's newer word
# as drift and wrote it back; for 7.5 h a host ran 5 runs against the owner's 3. Every limit change goes through
# this verb, so the verb carries the history: each row holds its why and old → new, a set prints the key's last
# change per host before writing, and refuses to undo a recent one another session made unless told --over.

RECENT = 24 * 3600
SKEW = 600   # a row this far ahead of the clock still counts as recent; one further ahead is a clock or a hand, never
             # a change that holds the key for a day past its time (the finish review)


def when(text):
    """An aware datetime from a row's `at` or a commit's %cI; None for anything else."""
    try:
        t = dt.datetime.fromisoformat(str(text).replace("Z", "+00:00"))
    except ValueError:
        return None
    return t if t.tzinfo else t.replace(tzinfo=dt.timezone.utc)


def fleet_rows(fl):
    """(rows, notes, unread): every row of limits.jsonl on each host of the fleet (this one's, each peer's over ssh),
    a row two hosts both hold counted once; a host whose file could not be read is a note and in UNREAD (a file that
    is there and will not read included, never taken for an empty one: the finish review)."""
    seen, rows, notes, unread = set(), [], [], set()
    for h in roadless(fl):
        notes.append("limits.jsonl on %s not read (no road from here: a change made there is unseen)" % h)
    for h, alias in fl.items():
        if alias is None:
            try:
                text = log_file().read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")
            except FileNotFoundError:
                text = ""
            except OSError as e:
                notes.append("limits.jsonl on %s not read (%s)" % (h, e))
                unread.add(h)
                continue
        else:
            try:
                text = run_ssh(alias, 'f=%s; if [ -e "$f" ]; then cat "$f" 2>/dev/null || echo @@unread; fi; echo @@end'
                               % STATE)
            except OSError as e:
                notes.append("limits.jsonl on %s not read (%s)" % (h, e))
                unread.add(h)
                continue
            if "@@end" not in text or "@@unread" in text:
                notes.append("limits.jsonl on %s not read (%s)" % (h, "no answer" if "@@end" not in text else
                                                                   "it is there and will not read"))
                unread.add(h)
                continue
            text = text.rsplit("@@end", 1)[0]
        for l in text.splitlines():
            try:
                r = json.loads(l)
            except ValueError:
                continue
            if not isinstance(r, dict) or when(r.get("at")) is None:
                continue
            if any(not isinstance(r.get(f), (str, type(None))) for f in ("by", "host", "key", "word", "session", "why",
                                                                         "old", "new", "line", "verb")):
                continue   # a hand-edited row of another shape: skipped, never a crash of every verb (round 1)
            sig = json.dumps(r, sort_keys=True)
            if sig not in seen:
                seen.add(sig)
                rows.append(r)
    return rows, notes, unread


def same_target(r, key, h, w):
    """A row for KEY on host H, word W (ANY: any word of a login key)."""
    if r.get("key") != key or norm_host(r.get("host")) != h:
        return False
    return w is ANY or (r.get("word") or None) == (w or None)


ANY = object()


def row_changes(rows, key, h, w):
    """[change] for the target, oldest first: {at, by, session, old, new, why, src}. A row from before this change
    holds no old or new: its new is its line's value (an unset's: the config's, unknown), its old the row before's new.
    A change that moved nothing (old equal to new: a value set again) is left out: as the last change it would hide
    the real one under it from the guard (round 1). ANY: each word its own series, their last changes (round 1)."""
    if w is ANY:
        words = sorted({r.get("word") or "" for r in rows if same_target(r, key, h, ANY)})
        lasts = [c for x in words for c in row_changes(rows, key, h, x or None)[-1:]]
        return sorted(lasts, key=lambda c: c["at"])
    out, prev = [], None
    for r in sorted((r for r in rows if same_target(r, key, h, w)), key=lambda r: when(r["at"])):
        line, word = r.get("line"), r.get("word")
        new = r["new"] if "new" in r else (None if line is None else (word_of(line)[1] if word else line))
        old = r["old"] if "old" in r else prev
        prev = new
        if old is not None and new is not None and canon(old) == canon(new):
            continue
        out.append({"at": when(r["at"]), "by": r.get("by") or "?", "session": r.get("session") or None,
                    "old": old, "new": new, "why": r.get("why"), "src": "row", "verb": r.get("verb"),
                    "over": r.get("over") is True})
    return out


_OVERLAY = {}


def overlay_commits(key):
    """(commits newest first, why not read): [(sha, at, subject, old tokens or None, new tokens or None)] for each
    overlay commit that changed KEY's row (`<key> <values…>`, as the overlay's config table holds it), read with
    `git log -G` over the files that hold the row today (else every file that ever held it), each dated by when it
    was committed (%cI: a rebased commit keeps an older author date). (None, why) when an overlay is named but cannot be read;
    ([], None) when none is named. Cached per key."""
    if key in _OVERLAY:
        return _OVERLAY[key]
    root = common.overlay_root()
    if root is None:
        _OVERLAY[key] = ([], None)
        return _OVERLAY[key]
    pat = "^%s( |$)" % key   # KEY_RE's characters only: nothing to escape
    git = ["git", "-C", str(root)]
    try:
        # HEAD and its upstream as last fetched: a drop committed on the other Mac and pushed, not yet fast-forwarded
        # here, is read too (the finish review)
        u = subprocess.run(git + ["rev-parse", "--verify", "-q", "@{u}"], capture_output=True, text=True,
                           errors="replace", timeout=20, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL)
        revs = ["HEAD"] + (["@{u}"] if u.returncode == 0 and u.stdout.strip() else [])
        g = subprocess.run(git + ["grep", "-l", "-E", pat] + revs + ["--"], capture_output=True, text=True,
                           errors="replace", timeout=20, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL)
        if g.returncode not in (0, 1):
            raise OSError((g.stderr.strip().splitlines() or ["exit %d" % g.returncode])[-1][:160])
        files = sorted({l.split(":", 1)[1] for l in g.stdout.splitlines() if ":" in l})
        if not files:
            # no file holds the row today: it may have been dropped whole, the owner's word as much as a changed
            # value (round 1); the files any commit changed it in, over the whole history (about 1.4 s on the overlay)
            f = subprocess.run(git + ["log", "--format=", "--name-only", "-G", pat] + revs, capture_output=True,
                               text=True, errors="replace", timeout=20, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL)
            if f.returncode != 0:
                raise OSError((f.stderr.strip().splitlines() or ["exit %d" % f.returncode])[-1][:160])
            files = sorted({l.strip() for l in f.stdout.splitlines() if l.strip()})
        if not files:
            _OVERLAY[key] = ([], None)
            return _OVERLAY[key]
        r = subprocess.run(git + ["log", "-p", "--no-color", "--no-ext-diff", "-U0",
                                  "--format=@@commit%x09%H%x09%cI%x09%s", "-G", pat] + revs + ["--"] + files,
                           capture_output=True, text=True, errors="replace", timeout=20, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL)
        if r.returncode != 0:
            raise OSError((r.stderr.strip().splitlines() or ["exit %d" % r.returncode])[-1][:160])
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as e:
        _OVERLAY[key] = (None, "the overlay's history at %s not read (%s)" % (root, e))
        return _OVERLAY[key]
    out = []
    for l in r.stdout.splitlines():
        if l.startswith("@@commit\t"):
            _, sha, at, subject = (l.split("\t", 3) + [""])[:4]
            out.append([sha, when(at), subject, None, None])
        elif out and l[:1] in "-+" and re.match(r"^[-+]%s( |$)" % re.escape(key), l):
            toks = l[1:].split()[1:]
            out[-1][3 if l[0] == "-" else 4] = toks
    _OVERLAY[key] = ([tuple(c) for c in out if c[1] is not None], None)
    return _OVERLAY[key]


def row_value(spec, h, w, toks):
    """What a host reads from the overlay's row TOKENS (None: no row) for word W: its entry, a `*:` entry where the key
    takes one, else the default."""
    ents = common.config_entries(toks or [])
    if spec["shape"] == "plain":
        return " ".join(v for x, v in ents if x is None) or spec["default"] or "none"
    if w is ANY:
        return " ".join(toks or []) or "none"
    for want in (w, "*") if (spec["star"] or spec["shape"] == "login") else (w,):
        for x, v in ents:
            if x == want:
                return v
    return spec["default"] or "none"


def overlay_change(key, spec, h, w):
    """(change or None, note or None): the newest overlay commit that moved what host H reads for W."""
    commits, why = overlay_commits(key)
    if commits is None:
        return None, why
    for sha, at, subject, old, new in commits:
        o, n = row_value(spec, h, w, old), row_value(spec, h, w, new)
        if canon(o) != canon(n):
            return {"at": at, "by": "the overlay's %s" % sha[:8], "session": None, "old": o, "new": n,
                    "why": subject, "src": "overlay", "verb": "commit"}, None
    return None, None


def last_change(key, spec, h, w, rows):
    """(the newest change for the target, from the fleet's rows or the overlay, or None; a note or None)."""
    ch = row_changes(rows, key, h, w)
    ov, note = overlay_change(key, spec, h, w)
    cands = ([ch[-1]] if ch else []) + ([ov] if ov else [])
    return (max(cands, key=lambda c: c["at"]) if cands else None), note


def judged(key, spec, h, w, rows, cur, base):
    """(the change a write to the target is judged against, or None; a note or None): newest first over the target's
    rows and the overlay's commit, this session's own rows passed over, so its own newer change never hides another
    session's under it (the finish review: two steps undid it without --over), and its own --over row ends the walk
    (that row answered what lay under it). A login whose host reads the `*:` line (no line of its own in the layer
    or the config) is moved by a `*:` change too, so that series counts for it."""
    cands = list(row_changes(rows, key, h, w))
    if spec["shape"] == "login" and w not in (None, "*"):
        if not any(x == w for x, _ in common.config_entries(common.live_merge(cur, base) or [])):
            cands += row_changes(rows, key, h, "*")
    ov, note = overlay_change(key, spec, h, w)
    # newest first, a tie in whole seconds taken in the order the rows were written (sorted is stable; reverse=True
    # would keep the older of a tie first)
    for c in reversed(sorted(cands + ([ov] if ov else []), key=lambda c: c["at"])):
        if another(c):
            return c, note
        if c.get("over"):
            return None, note
    return None, note


def recent(c, now):
    return -SKEW < (now - c["at"]).total_seconds() < RECENT


def age(t):
    s = max(0, int((dt.datetime.now(dt.timezone.utc) - t).total_seconds()))
    return "%d min" % (s // 60) if s < 3600 else "%d h" % (s // 3600) if s < 2 * 86400 else "%d d" % (s // 86400)


def who(c):
    if c["src"] == "overlay":
        return c["by"]
    return "%s, %s" % (c["by"], "session " + c["session"][:8] if c["session"] else "no session")


def show_value(v):
    return "the config's" if v is None else v


def said(c):
    """One change, whole: when, from whom, old → new, its why."""
    return "%s (%s ago) by %s: %s → %s — %s" % (
        c["at"].astimezone().strftime("%Y-%m-%d %H:%M %Z").strip(), age(c["at"]), who(c),
        "?" if c["old"] is None else c["old"], show_value(c["new"]), c["why"] or "no why recorded")


def brief(c):
    """One change on a list line."""
    if c is None:
        return "last: none on record"
    return "last: %s ago by %s, %s → %s%s" % (age(c["at"]), who(c), "?" if c["old"] is None else c["old"],
                                              show_value(c["new"]), ", \"%s\"" % c["why"] if c["why"] else "")


def num(v):
    try:
        return float(v)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return None


def undoes(c, new):
    """True when writing NEW moves the value the other way from change C: from C's new value, not from what the host
    reads now, which may still be the config C replaced (an overlay commit not yet deployed: round 2). Both numbers and
    the other direction from C's new; else NEW is C's old value back."""
    if c is None or c["new"] is None or canon(new) == canon(c["new"]):
        return False
    a, b, y = num(c["old"]), num(c["new"]), num(new)
    if None not in (a, b, y):
        return (b - a) * (y - b) < 0
    # no direction to read (a login's first line, its old `none`; a rule such as `1.5 log`): any other value moves
    # it, held as an undo rather than passed blind (the finish review)
    return True


def my_session():
    """This session's id: Claude Code's, else Codex's (helmlib reads the same), else None (a terminal)."""
    return os.environ.get("CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID") or os.environ.get("CODEX_SESSION_ID") or None


def another(c):
    """Whether change C came from another session than this one: an overlay commit always; a row, unless its
    session is this one, or neither has a session and it was made from this host (one terminal's two sets)."""
    if c["src"] == "overlay":
        return True
    mine = my_session()
    if mine or c["session"]:
        return c["session"] != mine
    return norm_host(c["by"]) != me()


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- what a host reads

def word_of(line):
    m = re.match(r"^([^\s:#(]+):\s*(.*)$", line)
    return (m.group(1).lower(), m.group(2).strip()) if m else (None, line)


def effective(spec, host, layer, base):
    """(value, where from) as the key's reader on host takes it."""
    merged = common.live_merge(layer, base) or []
    from_layer = set(layer or [])
    if spec["shape"] == "login":
        if not merged:
            return "none", "default (the registry's)"
        return " ".join(merged), "live" if layer else "config"
    if spec["shape"] == "plain":
        if not merged:
            return spec["default"] or "none", "default"
        return " ".join(merged), "live" if layer else "config"
    ents = common.config_entries(merged)
    live_words = {w for w, _ in common.config_entries(layer)}
    for want in (host, "*") if spec["star"] else (host,):
        for w, v in ents:
            if w == want:
                where = "live" if w in live_words else "config"
                return v, where + ("" if want == host else " (*:)")
    return spec["default"] or "none", "default"


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- the trial read

def canon(text):
    """Tokens compared as the readers print them: numbers as numbers, `k=v` with v a number, in any order."""
    out = []
    for t in re.split(r"[\s,]+", (text or "").strip()):
        if not t:
            continue
        k, eq, v = t.partition("=")
        try:
            out.append("%s=%r" % (k, float(v)) if eq else repr(float(t)))
        except ValueError:
            out.append(t.lower())
    return sorted(out)


def trial(key, spec, host, line):
    """None when the key's reader, on a config dir holding only `line` in its live layer, reads it back;
    else why not."""
    w, v = word_of(line)
    login = w if spec["shape"] == "login" else ""
    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory(prefix="regatta-limit-") as t:
        f = Path(t) / common.LIVE / key
        f.parent.mkdir(parents=True)
        f.write_text(line + "\n", encoding="utf-8")
        env = {k: x for k, x in os.environ.items() if k not in TRIAL_DROP}
        env["REGATTA_CONFIG"] = t
        cmd = probe_cmd(spec, host, login)
        try:
            # no stdin of the caller's, as run_ssh: a probe must not eat the rest of a `while read` loop's file
            r = subprocess.run(["/bin/sh", "-c", cmd], cwd=str(spec["module"]), env=env, capture_output=True,
                               text=True, timeout=60, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL)
        except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as e:
            return "its reader did not answer (%s)" % type(e).__name__
    said = (r.stderr.strip().splitlines() or [""])[-1][:200]
    got = re.sub(r"\s*\(.*\)\s*$", "", (r.stdout.strip().splitlines() or [""])[0])
    if r.returncode != 0:
        return "%s (exit %d%s)" % (refusal(r.returncode), r.returncode, ": " + said if said else "")
    if not canon(got):
        # a headroom line with no rules (`host-a:,`) reads back as nothing, which canon(",") also is: refused, as
        # get would call the same reading a refusal (the landing review)
        return "its reader reads nothing"
    if canon(got) != canon(v):
        return "its reader reads %s, not %s%s" % (got or "nothing", v, " (" + said + ")" if said else "")
    if said and got.strip() != v.strip() and re.search(r"\bdefault\b", said):
        # the same number in another spelling (`03`, `3.0`) that the reader refused aloud and met with a default
        # equal to it: dispatch's cap_here says `is no cap … the default here` and reads 3 (the round-1 review of the
        # laptop run). Only a reader that says it took its default: headroom names its source line on stderr every
        # time, and `rate=50,avail=20` is its own line in another order (round 2)
        return "its reader reads %s only by its default (%s)" % (got, said)
    return None


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- the verbs

def probe_cmd(spec, host, word=None):
    return spec["probe"].replace("{host}", shlex.quote(host)).replace("{login}", shlex.quote(word or "*"))


def refusal(rc):
    """What a reader's non-zero exit says. The readers refuse with 2; any other code (python's 1 on a traceback, 127
    for a missing interpreter, a signal) is a reader that could not tell, never a verdict on the config (AGENTS.md's
    trap: a probe's refuse exit must not be 1; the landing review)."""
    return "its reader refuses it" if rc == 2 else "its reader could not tell"


def answer(rc, out):
    """(value, why) from a reader's exit code and output: (None, why) when it refuses or gives nothing."""
    got = re.sub(r"\s*\(.*\)\s*$", "", (out.strip().splitlines() or [""])[0])
    if rc != 0:
        return None, ("its reader refuses the config as merged (exit %d)" if rc == 2
                      else "its reader could not tell (exit %d)") % rc
    return (got, None) if got else (None, "its reader reads nothing")


def read_back(key, spec, host, word=None):
    """(value, why) the key's reader on this host reads now, from the real config dir (no scratch): the ground truth
    a file's text is not, an override in this environment included."""
    env = {k: x for k, x in os.environ.items() if k not in HOST_VARS}
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["/bin/sh", "-c", probe_cmd(spec, host, word)], cwd=str(spec["module"]), env=env,
                           capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as e:
        return None, "its reader did not answer (%s)" % type(e).__name__
    return answer(r.returncode, r.stdout)


def peer_reads(alias, host, items):
    """{i: (value, why)} for items [(key, spec, word)], each asked of the key's reader on the peer, in one ssh: the
    peer's own checkout through its config dir's `home` file, as berth's probe reaches it. A peer with no home, or a
    checkout without the module, answers (None, None): nothing of regatta there to ask (the header: a host needs
    nothing installed to be written). Raises OSError when the peer cannot be reached (the round-1 review: a set or
    get on a peer read the file's text alone, so a checkout that predates the layer read as `live`)."""
    parts = [REMOTE_DIR, 'r="$(head -1 "$c/home" 2>/dev/null)"']
    for i, (key, spec, word) in enumerate(items):
        if not re.match(r"^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*$", spec["name"]):
            continue
        # a checkout without the module's `limits` file predates the live layer: its readers lack the verbs
        # the probes call, so its exit would read as a refusal (round 2)
        parts.append('echo @@q%d; if [ -z "$r" ] || [ ! -d "$r/modules/%s" ]; then echo @@none; '
                     'elif [ ! -f "$r/modules/%s/limits" ]; then echo @@old; else (cd "$r/modules/%s" && %s) '
                     '</dev/null 2>/dev/null; echo "@@rc$?"; fi'
                     % (i, spec["name"], spec["name"], spec["name"], probe_cmd(spec, host, word)))
    parts.append("echo @@end")
    out = run_ssh(alias, "; ".join(parts))
    if "@@end" not in out:
        raise OSError("no answer from %s" % alias)
    got = {}
    for i in range(len(items)):
        if "@@q%d\n" % i not in out:
            got[i] = (None, None)
            continue
        part = out.split("@@q%d\n" % i, 1)[1]
        part = re.split(r"@@q\d+\n|@@end", part, maxsplit=1)[0]
        if part.startswith("@@none"):
            got[i] = (None, None)
            continue
        if part.startswith("@@old"):
            got[i] = (None, OLD)
            continue
        m = re.search(r"@@rc(\d+)\s*$", part)
        got[i] = answer(int(m.group(1)), part[:m.start()]) if m else (None, "its reader did not answer")
    return got


OLD = "its checkout predates the live layer and reads the config until it updates"


def reads(host, alias, items):
    """{i: (value, why)} of each item's reader on host, this one or a peer."""
    if alias is None:
        return {i: read_back(k, sp, host, w) for i, (k, sp, w) in enumerate(items)}
    return peer_reads(alias, host, items)


def show(keys, tab, hosts, fl, rows):
    bad = 0
    here = me()
    for h in hosts:
        try:
            got = read_host_keys(h, fl[h], keys)
        except OSError as e:
            rows += [(k, h, "?", "could not reach %s: %s" % (h, e)) for k in keys]
            bad = 1
            continue
        # each host's reader's own answer, a peer's included (its checkout through its home file), so a line it
        # ignores (a cap of 0, a typo, a checkout that predates the layer) shows as what it does, and one it
        # refuses shows refused (the round-1 review)
        asked = [k for k in keys if tab[k]["shape"] != "login"]
        unasked = None
        try:
            ans = reads(h, fl[h], [(k, tab[k], None) for k in asked])
        except OSError as e:
            ans, unasked = {}, "not read back (%s)" % e
        ans = {k: ans.get(i, (None, unasked)) for i, k in enumerate(asked)}
        for key in keys:
            spec = tab[key]
            layer, base = got[key]
            v, where = effective(spec, h, layer, base)
            rb, why = ans.get(key, (None, None))
            if why == OLD:
                # that host reads the config, not the layer: its value column is the config's (the landing review)
                v, where = effective(spec, h, None, base)
            if rb is not None and canon(rb) != canon(v):
                where += "; its reader reads %s" % rb
                v = rb
            elif why in (OLD, unasked) and why:
                where += "; " + why   # said, as a peer with no checkout is: the value is written, unread there
            elif why:
                where += "; " + why
                bad = 1
            elif rb is None and fl[h] is not None and key in asked:
                where += "; not read back (no regatta checkout there)"
            rows.append((key, h, v, where))
    return bad


def cmd_list(a, tab, keys):
    fl = fleet()
    hosts = hosts_of(a, fl)
    rows, bad = [], 0
    bad |= show(keys, tab, hosts, fl, rows)
    # each value's last change beside it (limit-history): the fleet's rows read once, the overlay once per key
    hist, notes, _ = fleet_rows(fl)
    for n in notes:
        print("regatta limit: %s" % n, file=sys.stderr)
    said_notes = set()
    lasts = []
    for k, h, _, _ in rows:
        sh = tab[k]["shape"]
        c, note = last_change(k, tab[k], h, h if sh == "host" else ANY if sh == "login" else None, hist)
        if note and note not in said_notes:
            said_notes.add(note)
            print("regatta limit: %s" % note, file=sys.stderr)
        lasts.append(brief(c))
    wk = max([len(r[0]) for r in rows] + [3])
    wh = max([len(r[1]) for r in rows] + [4])
    wv = max([len(r[2]) for r in rows] + [5])
    for r, last in zip(rows, lasts):
        print("%-*s  %-*s  %-*s  %s · %s" % (wk, r[0], wh, r[1], wv, r[2], r[3], last))
    return 3 if bad else 0


def plan_set(a, key, spec, fl):
    """[(host, word or None, line)] to write: word None for a plain key."""
    shape = spec["shape"]
    out = []
    if a.all or a.on:
        hosts = hosts_of(a, fl, key if spec["shape"] == "host" else None)
        for item in a.values:
            w, v = word_of(item)
            if shape == "login":
                if not w:
                    raise Usage("%s takes LOGIN:VALUE (`*:` for any login)" % key)
                out += [(h, w, "%s:%s" % (w, v)) for h in hosts]
            elif w:
                raise Usage("with --all or --on, %s takes a bare VALUE: each host gets its own line" % key)
            else:
                out += [(h, h if shape == "host" else None, "%s:%s" % (h, item) if shape == "host" else item)
                        for h in hosts]
        if len(a.values) != 1 and shape != "login":
            raise Usage("with --all or --on, one VALUE")
    else:
        if shape == "login":
            raise Usage("%s is a login's: name the hosts it is set on (--on H[,H…] or --all)" % key)
        for item in a.values:
            w, v = word_of(item)
            if not w or w == "*":
                raise Usage("%s takes HOST:VALUE (or VALUE --all)" % key)
            h = norm_host(w)
            if h not in fl:
                raise Usage("no road to %s: not this host, nor a peer regatta/fleet-ssh (else wharf-runs/ssh) names" % h)
            out.append((h, h if shape == "host" else None, "%s:%s" % (h, v) if shape == "host" else v))
    for _, w, line in out:
        _, v = word_of(line) if w else (None, line)
        if not v.strip() or "\n" in v:
            raise Usage("an empty value: `regatta limit unset` takes a value away")
    return out


def stands_over(spec, w, base):
    """What a value for word W stands over, whole: W's entry in the config, on whatever line; for a key a `*:` line
    serves, that entry where W has none; else the default (rounds 3 and 4 of the review)."""
    ents = common.config_entries(base)
    if not w:
        return ["the config's " + " ".join(v for x, v in ents if x is None)] if any(x is None for x, _ in ents) else []
    own = ["the config's %s:%s" % (x, v) for x, v in ents if x == w]
    if own or spec is None or spec["shape"] != "host":
        return own[:1]
    star = ["the config's *:%s" % v for x, v in ents if x == "*"][:1] if spec["star"] else []
    return star or (["the default " + spec["default"]] if spec["default"] else [])


def lock_here():
    """An exclusive lock on this host's layer while one set here reads, edits and writes it: two sets at once would
    otherwise lose one. A set into a peer reads and writes in two ssh calls, unlocked (the digest's kept finding)."""
    import fcntl
    f = common.CONFIG / common.LIVE / ".lock"
    try:
        f.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        fh = open(f, "a")
    except OSError:
        return None
    try:
        fcntl.flock(fh, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
    except OSError:
        fh.close()
        return None
    return fh


def value_for(spec, h, w, layer, base):
    """What host H's reader takes for word W (None for a plain key) from LAYER over BASE: a login key's own line,
    else its `*:` line, else none; any other key as effective() reads it."""
    if spec["shape"] != "login":
        return effective(spec, h, layer, base)[0]
    ents = common.config_entries(common.live_merge(layer, base) or [])
    for want in (w, "*"):
        for x, v in ents:
            if x == want:
                return v
    return "none"


def edit_layer(layer, items, key=None, h=None):
    """LAYER with ITEMS [(word, line or None)] applied: a line replaces its word's (a plain key's, the whole layer),
    None takes it away. With KEY and H, an unset with nothing to take is said."""
    new = list(layer or [])
    for w, line in items:
        if key and line is None and not any((word_of(l)[0] == w) if w else True for l in new):
            print("regatta limit: %s on %s has no live line%s to unset" % (key, h, " for " + w if w else ""),
                  file=sys.stderr)
        if w is None:   # a plain key: the value is the whole layer
            new = [] if line is None else [line]
        else:
            new = [l for l in new if word_of(l)[0] != w] + ([] if line is None else [line])
    return new


def guard(key, spec, fl, plan, verb, over):
    """Before a set or unset writes: each target's last change printed (when, by whom, old → new, why) from the
    fleet's limits.jsonl and the overlay's history; refused (exit 2, nothing written) where another session's change
    under 24 h old (judged()) would be moved the other way, unless OVER. An unset is held by the same rule: taking a
    recent value away undoes it as a set would; one that changes nothing the host reads is not. A target whose own
    limits.jsonl will not read is held the same way, never judged blind; one whose layer will not read is not written
    at all (SKIP), so a read that fails here and answers in apply never writes past the guard (the finish review).
    (exit or None, SKIP {host: why}, whether --over was used)."""
    rows, notes, unread = fleet_rows(fl)
    for n in notes:
        print("regatta limit: %s" % n, file=sys.stderr)
    now = dt.datetime.now(dt.timezone.utc)
    by = {}
    for h, w, line in plan:
        by.setdefault(h, []).append((w, line))
    refused, said_notes, skip = [], set(), {}
    for h, items in by.items():
        try:
            layer, base = read_host(h, fl[h], key)
        except OSError as e:
            skip[h] = str(e)   # apply says it could not be reached; the history is still printed
            layer = base = ()
        if h in unread and h not in skip:
            refused.append("%s on %s: its own limits.jsonl could not be read, so a change made there is unseen"
                           % (key, h))
        cur = list(layer or [])
        for w, line in items:
            target = w if spec["shape"] != "plain" else None
            c, note = last_change(key, spec, h, target, rows)
            if note and note not in said_notes:
                said_notes.add(note)
                print("regatta limit: %s" % note, file=sys.stderr)
            print("%s on %s%s: %s" % (key, h, " for " + w if spec["shape"] == "login" else "",
                                      "last changed " + said(c) if c else "no change on record"))
            if h in skip:
                continue
            nxt = edit_layer(cur, [(w, line)])
            old, new = value_for(spec, h, w, cur, base), value_for(spec, h, w, nxt, base)
            j, _ = judged(key, spec, h, target, rows, cur, base)
            prev, cur = cur, nxt
            # a write that leaves the layer as it was changes nothing, whatever it reads (an unset with no line); an
            # unset that leaves the value the host reads as it was is cleanup, never an undo (the finish review)
            if nxt == prev or (verb == "unset" and canon(old) == canon(new)):
                continue
            if j and recent(j, now) and undoes(j, new):
                refused.append("%s on %s: %s → %s would undo the change of %s" % (key, h, old, new, said(j)))
    if not refused:
        return None, skip, False
    if over:
        for r in refused:
            print("regatta limit: --over: %s" % r, file=sys.stderr)
        return None, skip, True
    for r in refused:
        print("regatta limit: refused %s %s — %s; nothing written. Ask whoever made it, or pass --over with a --why "
              "that answers it" % (verb, key, r), file=sys.stderr)
    return 2, skip, False


def apply(key, fl, plan, verb, spec, why, skip=None, overrode=False):
    """Each host's layer rewritten for its items; (done, unreached). Each item's row holds WHY, the values its layer
    and config give before and after (the history guard() reads), and `over` when --over carried it past a refusal.
    A host in SKIP (guard could not read it) is unreached, never written."""
    by = {}
    for h, w, line in plan:
        by.setdefault(h, []).append((w, line))
    done, unreached = [], []
    for h, items in by.items():
        if skip and h in skip:
            unreached.append(h)
            print("regatta limit: could not reach %s (%s): %s — its value is unchanged" % (h, fl[h], skip[h]),
                  file=sys.stderr)
            continue
        held = lock_here() if fl[h] is None else None
        try:
            layer, base = read_host(h, fl[h], key)
            for w, line in items:   # what the value stands over, whole (the header): said, never merged
                if line is None:
                    continue
                over = stands_over(spec, w, base)
                if over and over != ["the config's " + line]:   # its own value again: nothing to say
                    print("regatta limit: %s on %s: %s stands over %s, whole" % (key, h, line, " ".join(over)),
                          file=sys.stderr)
            moves, cur = [], list(layer or [])
            for w, line in items:
                nxt = edit_layer(cur, [(w, line)], key, h)
                moves.append((value_for(spec, h, w, cur, base), value_for(spec, h, w, nxt, base)))
                cur = nxt
            new = cur
            write_host(h, fl[h], key, new)
        except OSError as e:
            if held:
                held.close()
            unreached.append(h)
            print("regatta limit: could not reach %s (%s): %s — its value is unchanged" % (h, fl[h], e), file=sys.stderr)
            continue
        if held:
            held.close()
        done.append(h)
        for (w, line), (old, new) in zip(items, moves):
            row = {"at": dt.datetime.now(dt.timezone.utc).isoformat(timespec="seconds"), "by": me(), "host": h,
                   "key": key, "verb": verb, "line": line, "word": w, "why": why, "old": old, "new": new,
                   "session": my_session()}
            if overrode:
                row["over"] = True
            log(row)
            if fl[h] is not None:
                log_on(fl[h], row)   # the same row on the host changed, which a fleet read counts once
            print("%s %s on %s: %s" % (verb, key, h, line if line is not None else (w or "the value") + " → the config's"))
    return done, unreached


def verify(key, spec, plan, fl, done):
    """After the writes: each written host's reader reads the value set, or say what it reads (exit 3). A peer's is
    asked over ssh through its home file (the round-1 review); a peer with nothing of regatta to ask is said, not
    failed. A host not written (unreached) is not asked: its message is said already."""
    bad = 0
    by = {}
    for h, w, line in plan:
        if h in done and line is not None:
            by.setdefault(h, []).append((w, line))
    for h, items in by.items():
        try:
            ans = reads(h, fl[h], [(key, spec, w if spec["shape"] == "login" else None) for w, _ in items])
        except OSError as e:
            print("regatta limit: %s on %s: written, but its reader could not be asked (%s)" % (key, h, e),
                  file=sys.stderr)
            bad = 1
            continue
        for i, (w, line) in enumerate(items):
            want = word_of(line)[1] if w else line
            rb, why = ans.get(i, (None, None))
            if rb is None and why is None:
                print("regatta limit: %s on %s: written; no regatta checkout there to read it back" % (key, h),
                      file=sys.stderr)
            elif why == OLD:
                print("regatta limit: %s on %s: written, but %s" % (key, h, OLD), file=sys.stderr)
                bad = 1
            elif rb is None or canon(rb) != canon(want):
                print("regatta limit: %s on %s: written, but %s" % (key, h, "its reader reads %s, not %s (an override in "
                      "the environment, DISPATCH_MAX_RUNS or CHECK_SLOTS, or a checkout that predates the live layer?)"
                      % (rb, want) if rb else why + " — `regatta limit unset` takes the value back"), file=sys.stderr)
                bad = 1
    return bad


def cmd_set(a, tab):
    key = resolve_key(a.key, tab)
    spec = tab[key]
    fl = fleet()
    plan = plan_set(a, key, spec, fl)
    refused = []
    for h, w, line in plan:
        why = trial(key, spec, h, line)
        if why:
            refused.append("%s on %s: %s" % (line, h, why))
    if refused:
        for r in refused:
            print("regatta limit: refused %s %s — %s; nothing written" % (key, r.split(" on ", 1)[0], r.split(" on ", 1)[1]),
                  file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    held, skip, overrode = guard(key, spec, fl, plan, "set", a.over)
    if held:
        return held
    done, unreached = apply(key, fl, plan, "set", spec, a.why, skip, overrode)
    if spec["shape"] == "login" and any(w == "*" for _, w, _ in plan):
        print("regatta limit: a `*:` line serves only a login whose registry row sets no week ceiling of its own; "
              "name the login to stand over one (LOGIN:VALUE)", file=sys.stderr)
    if spec["shape"] == "login" and any(w not in (None, "*") for _, w, _ in plan):
        # a second home for a login's ceiling beside budgets.json (main, 2026-10-31): this line outranks it on these
        # hosts until unset, so say it at the set (the round-1 review of the laptop run)
        print("regatta limit: a login's line stands over its registry row's and budgets.json's week ceiling on these "
              "hosts until `regatta limit unset`", file=sys.stderr)
    unread = verify(key, spec, plan, fl, done)
    return 3 if unreached or unread else 0


def cmd_unset(a, tab):
    key = resolve_key(a.key, tab)
    spec = tab[key]
    fl = fleet()
    plan = []
    if spec["shape"] == "login":
        if not (a.all or a.on) or not a.values:
            raise Usage("%s: unset LOGIN [LOGIN…] (--on H[,H…] or --all)" % key)
        hosts = hosts_of(a, fl)
        plan = [(h, w.lower(), None) for h in hosts for w in a.values]
    else:
        if a.all or a.on:
            if a.values:
                raise Usage("unset %s takes hosts or --all/--on, not both" % key)
            hosts = hosts_of(a, fl)
        else:
            if not a.values:
                raise Usage("unset %s takes HOST [HOST…] or --all" % key)
            hosts = [norm_host(h) for h in a.values]
            bad = [h for h in hosts if h not in fl]
            if bad:
                raise Usage("no road to %s" % ", ".join(bad))
        plan = [(h, h if spec["shape"] == "host" else None, None) for h in hosts]
    held, skip, overrode = guard(key, spec, fl, plan, "unset", a.over)
    if held:
        return held
    _, unreached = apply(key, fl, plan, "unset", spec, a.why, skip, overrode)
    return 3 if unreached else 0


def main(argv=None):
    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="regatta limit", description=__doc__.split("\n\n")[0])
    sub = p.add_subparsers(dest="verb", required=True)
    for name in ("list", "get", "set", "unset"):
        s = sub.add_parser(name)
        if name != "list":
            s.add_argument("key")
        if name in ("set", "unset"):
            s.add_argument("values", nargs="*")
            s.add_argument("--why", help="one line: why this change (required; recorded in its row)")
            s.add_argument("--over", action="store_true",
                           help="write though it undoes another session's change of the last 24 h")
        g = s.add_mutually_exclusive_group()
        g.add_argument("--on", help="hosts, comma-separated")
        g.add_argument("--all", action="store_true", help="every host of the fleet")
    a = p.parse_args(argv)
    if a.verb in ("set", "unset") and common.unattended_caller():
        # an unattended caller never gets --over either: it is refused the whole verb, by the one rule that also
        # counts peer-start's AUTOFINISH_CALLER_UNATTENDED (the finish review)
        print("regatta limit: %s writes a host's live limits — refused under %s; the owner's session runs it"
              % (a.verb, common.unattended_caller()), file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if a.verb in ("set", "unset"):
        a.why = (a.why or "").strip()
        if not a.why or "\n" in a.why or "\r" in a.why:
            print("regatta limit: %s takes --why TEXT, one line saying why: it is recorded with the change, and the "
                  "next set of this key prints it" % a.verb, file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
    tab = table()
    try:
        if a.verb == "list":
            return cmd_list(a, tab, sorted(tab))
        if a.verb == "get":
            return cmd_list(a, tab, [resolve_key(a.key, tab)])
        if a.verb == "set":
            if not a.values:
                raise Usage("set %s takes HOST:VALUE, or VALUE --all" % a.key)
            return cmd_set(a, tab)
        return cmd_unset(a, tab)
    except Usage as e:
        print("regatta limit: %s" % e, file=sys.stderr)
        return 2


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
